          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is commonly called
            %em geometric realization
            (or, less commonly but more accurately:
            = succeed ",see" do
              %em topological realization
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#OnTerminology"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X|
            obtained by interpreting each element in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n
            – each abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-simplex"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-simplex
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            – as one copy of the standard topological
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi Top
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^n_{Top}
            and then gluing together all these along their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundaries"} boundaries
            to a big topological space, using the information encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/face+map"} face and degeneracy maps
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on how these simplices are supposed to be stuck together. This procedure generalises the geometric realization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complexes"} simplicial complexes
            as described at that entry.
          %p
            Geometric realization is the special case of the general notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve+and+realization"} nerve and realization
            that is induced from the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} cosimplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↦
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi Top
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n] \mapsto \Delta^n_{Top}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            denotes the ordinal with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            elements. The corresponding contravariant representable is denoted
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta(-, n)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+geometric+realization"} cubical geometric realization
          %p
            In the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            geometric realization plays a notable role in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
          %p
            The construction generalizes naturally to a map from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            to plain topological spaces. For more on that see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization+of+simplicial+spaces"} geometric realization of simplicial spaces
          %p
            The dual concept is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totalization"} totalization
            \.
          %p
          #OnTerminology.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (alternative terminology: “topological realization”)
              %br/
              While the term
              %em geometric realization
              is classical (
              %a{:href => "#Quillen68"}> Quillen 1968
              ) and in common use by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topologists
              (e.g.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#GoerssJardine09"} Goerss &amp; Jardine 2009, §VII.3
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              produced by the realization construction are not yet what
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometers
              would commonly regard as reflecting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} Geometry
              on a topological space is instead typically taken to be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifold"} differentiable manifold
              (e.g.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
              (such as
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+structure"} Riemannian structure
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+structure"} conformal structure
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+type"} weak
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              of the realization, which is the most non-geometric aspect of any notion of “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"}> space
              ”. This becomes a real issue in contexts of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
              and more generally of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+theory"} geometric homotopy theory
              (such as for
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+theories"} differential cohomology theories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+triangulation"} equivariant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/triangulations"} triangulations
              of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %p
              In conclusion, a more accurate and more descriptive term for the realization operation discussed here would be
              = succeed "." do
                %strong topological realization
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Simpson96"} Simpson 1996
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "simplicial+approximation+theorem#Jardine04"} Jardine 2004
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Lackenby08"} Lackenby 2008, §I.2
              %a{:href => "operad#FresseHOGTG"} Fresse 2017
              the term “topological realization” is used in the pdf draft but is replaced by “geometric realization” in the published version.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p There are various levels of generality in which the notion of (topological) geometric realization makes sense. The basic definition is
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OfSimplicialSets"}> For cell complexes such as simplicial sets
              \.
          %p A generalization of this of central importance is the
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OfSimplicialTopologicalSpaces"} Geometric realization of simplicial topological spaces
          %p Up to homotopy, this is a special case of a general notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OfCohesiveInfinityGroupoids"}> Geometric realization of cohesive ∞-groupoids
              \.
          %p At the point-set level, it is also a special case of a general notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OfSimplicialObjects"}> Geometric realization of simplicial objects
              \.
          %h3#OfSimplicialSets Of cell complexes such as simplicial sets
          %p
