          %h3#a_construction_of_drinfeld_of_geometric_realization_as_hom01 A construction of Drinfeld of geometric realization as Hom([0,1],-)
          %p
            %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0304064"} Drinfeld, On the notion of geometric realization
            provides a conceptual explanation of preserving finite limits, and “reformulates the definitions so that the following facts become obvious:
          %ul
            %li
              %p geometric realization commutes with finite projective limits (e.g., with Cartesian products);
            %li
              %p
                the geometric realization of a simplicial set … (resp. cyclic set) is equipped with an action of the group of orientation preserving homeomorphisms of the segment
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I := [0, 1]
                … (resp. the circle
                = succeed ").\u201C" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^ 1
          %p
            A draft
            %a{:href => "http://mishap.sdf.org/Skorokhod_Geometric_Realisation.pdf"} M.Gavrilovich, K.Pimenov. Geometric realisation as a Skorokhod semi-continuous path space endofunctor
            attempts to further reformulate this by showing that, in a certain precise sense, geometric realisation is an endofunctor of a certain category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF
            of simplicial sets equipped with extra structure of topological nature (a notion of smallness). The underlying endofunctor of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSets
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSets
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi HHom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi preorders
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HHom ( Hom_{preorders}(-, [0,1]_\leq), Y_.)
          %p
            The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF
            contains simplicial sets, topological and uniform spaces as full subcategories, and has forgetful functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sF
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSets
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF\to sSets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sF
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF\to Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sF
                  %mo →
                  %mi UniformSpaces
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF\to UniformSpaces
            such that the following compositions are identity:
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sSets
                    %mo →
                    %mi sF
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSets
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSets\to sF\to sSets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi sF
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top\to sF\to Top
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi UniformSpaces
                    %mo →
                    %mi sF
                    %mo →
                    %mi UniformSpaces
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} UniformSpaces\to sF\to UniformSpaces
          %p
            Here are some details. The category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF
            may be thought as the category of simplicial sets with extra structure of topological nature, a notion of smallness. Formally it is just the category of simplicial objects in the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filters"} filters
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi HHom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi preorders
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi sF
                  %mo →
                  %mi sF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} HHom ( Hom_{preorders}(-, [0,1]_\leq), Y_.):sF\to sF
            above is the inner hom of sSets equipped with an extra structure motivated by Skorokhod/Levi-Prokhorov convergence. The precise claim is that the geometric realisation of sSets factors as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSets
                  %mo →
                  %mi sF
                  %mo →
                  %mi sF
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSets  \to sF \to sF \to Top
          %p
            To gain some intuition, consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mo .
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_.=\Delta_n=Hom(-,[n])
            the standard simplex. Then by Remark 2.4.1-2 of
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/~dan/Courses/2003/Spring/416/GraysonKtheory.pdf"} Grayson
            the standard geometric simplex is the set of monotone functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0.1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0.1]\to [n]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi preorders
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi preorders
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{preorders}([0,1]_\leq), [n] ) = Hom ( Hom_{preorders}(-, [0,1]_\leq), \Delta_n )
          %p equipped with a metric
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} dist
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi inf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo :
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dist(f,g)=\inf \{ \epsilon: \forall x \exists y ( f(x)=f(y) ) \wedge  \forall y \exists x ( f(x)=g(y) ) \}
          %p
            reminiscent of Skorokhod metric in probability theory. Now instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_n
            take an arbitrary simplicial set, and rewrite the definition of Skorokhod convergence in terms of the notion of smallness in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sF
          %h3#geometric_realization_preserves_fibrations Geometric realization preserves fibrations
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The geometric realization of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibration"} Serre fibration
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              This is shown in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Quillen68"} Quillen 68
          %p
            This result implies that the geometric realization functor preserves all five classes of maps in a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+cofibrations"} acyclic cofibrations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/acyclic+fibrations"} acyclic fibrations
          %p
            In fact the geometric realization of a Kan fibration is even a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+fibration"} Hurewicz fibration
            (at least relative to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+category+of+spaces"} convenient category of spaces
            in which it lives). This follows from the fact that
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/a+Serre+fibration+between+CW-complexes+is+a+Hurewicz+fibration"} a Serre fibration between CW-complexes is a Hurewicz fibration
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FP90"} Fritch and Piccinini, Theorem 4.5.25
          %h3#induced_properties_of_the_fibrant_replacement Induced properties of the fibrant replacement
          %p
            The previous two sections show that the geometric realization preserves finite limits and fibrations. Since its right adjoint, the singular complex functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top \to sSet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            are fibrant and the adjunction is simplicially enriched, it follows that the composite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet \to Top \to sSet
            is a simplicially enriched
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement+functor"} fibrant replacement functor
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sSet
            that additionally preserves both finite limits and fibrations.
          %h3#geometric_realization_of_barycentric_subdivisions Geometric realization of barycentric subdivisions
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#FP1967"}> Fritsch–Puppe, 1967
              ;
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#F1974"} Fritsch 1974
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Sd
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |Sd X| \to |X|
            %p
              from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/barycentric+subdivision"} barycentric subdivision
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to the geometric realization of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Sd
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |Sd X|
              into a subdivision of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |X|
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\subset X
          %p
            For an expository account, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FP1990"} Fritsch–Piccinini
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              obtained by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(\mathbf{B}G)
              the corresponding simplicial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mi G
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi N
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathcal{B}G = |N\mathbf{B}G|
              %p
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                that is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                for
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
                = succeed "s)," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
